==Old English==

===Alternative spellings===
* [[burg]]

===Etymology===
From Germanic ''*burgs'', from Indo-European ''*bhərgh-'' ‘fortified elevation’.  Cognate with Old Frisian ''[[burch]]'', Old Saxon ''[[burg]]'' (Dutch ''[[burcht]]''), Old High German ''[[burg]]'' (German ''[[Burg]]'' ‘castle’), Old Norse ''[[borg]]'' (Swedish ''[[borg]]''), Gothic [[𐌱𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌲𐍃]] ‘town’.  The IE root is also the source of Celtic ''*brig-'' (Middle Irish ''brī'' ‘hill’, Welsh ''[[bera]]'' ‘stack’), Avestan ''brzant-'' ‘high’, Ancient Greek '''{{polytonic|[[πύργος]]}}''' ‘tower’.

